# Git File Lifecycle (VERY IMPORTANT): A file moves through these stages :-
Working Directory (our local repo)
↓
Staging Area (source control)
↓
Repository (main - remote/Github repo)


# Typical Daily Workflow :-
Write code
↓
Make several changes
↓
git add .
↓
git commit -m "Meaningful update"
↓
git push
That’s how developers usually work.


Important                Terms
Repository (Repo)	  Project folder tracked by Git
Commit	              Snapshot/save point
Branch	              Separate version of code
Merge	              Combine branches
Clone	              Copy repo from GitHub
Push	              Upload code to GitHub
Fetch                 Fetch downloads only.
Pull	              Downloads latest code + Merge
Remote	              Online GitHub repo
Local Repo	          Repo on your computer


# Important Git Commands Summary :-
Command	               Purpose
git init	            Start Git
git status	            Check changes
git add .	            Stage files
git commit -m ""	    Save snapshot
git log	                History
git branch	            View branches
git checkout	        Switch branch
git merge	            Merge branches
git push	            Upload
git pull	            Download + Merge
git clone	            Copy repo
git stash	            Temporary save


# STEPS TO USE GIT :- 

1. Git Setup :-
git config --global user.name 'your name(globally it will used everywhere)'
git config --global user.email 'your email Id '


# COMMANDS :-

2. git init :-
This creates hidden .git folder, Now Git starts tracking project.

3. git status :- 
Check Status

4. git add .  :-
Add all Files to Staging

5. git commit -m "Initial commit" :-
Commit message should explain changes.

6. git log / git log --oneline :-
Check Commit History

7. git branch feature-login :-
Create New Branch

git branch
All Local branches

git branch -r
All Remote branches

git branch -a
Both Local and Remote branches

8. git checkout feature-login / modern-way - git switch feature-login
Switch Branch

9. git checkout -b feature-login :-
Create + Switch Together

10. git branch :-
See All Branches, Current branch has *

11. git merge feature-login :-
Now feature code added to main.

12. .gitignore :-
Some files should NOT go to GitHub.
Examples: node_modules, .env, build folders 

PUSH LOCAL PROJECT TO GITHUB :-
S-1 : We have to create Repo on GitHub
S-2 : Connect Local Repo :-
13. git remote add origin https://github.com/VK18-HRSH/localrepo.git

PUSH CODE : For First Time :-
14. git push -u origin main
LOCAL → GITHUB
Upload local commits to remote GitHub repo
Git remembers : local main ↔ GitHub main
After That :- git push

15. git clone repo-url :-
Clone Repository, Download repo from GitHub

16. git pull origin main :-
GITHUB → LOCAL
Download + merge remote changes into local repo

17. git stash :-
Temporarily save unfinished work.

18. git stash pop :-
restore + remove stash

19. git stash apply :-
restore only

20. git stash list :-
See Stored Stashes

21. git reset --soft HEAD~1 :-
Soft Reset.

22. git reset HEAD~1 :-
Mixed Reset.

23. git reset --hard HEAD~1 :-
Hard Reset.

24. git revert HEAD :-
Creates a new commit instead of deleting previous commit but it deletes the code of previous commit.
You can see that previous commit in "git log"

# What Are -m, -M, -u This :-
These are called: flags / options, They modify command behavior.

1. -m :- means : message
git commit -m "message"

2. -u :- Meaning : Connect local branch with remote branch.
git push -u origin main

3. -M :- means : move/rename branch forcefully
git branch -M main ==> like Here: master → main

4. -v :- means : Shows verbose/view details.
git remote -v

5. -f :- means : Force push, Dangerous sometimes.
git push -f

6. -b :- means : Creates New Branch
git checkout -b feature-login

7. -d : use : for local branch deletion(IF MERGED)
git branch -d feature-login

8. -D : use : for local branch deletion(IF NOT MERGED MEANS FORCE DELETE)
git branch -D feature-login

# IF YOU EVER STUCK IN VIM EDITOR THEN SIMPLY DO THIS :
How To Exit :-
Simply press :-  q / ! / :qw / :q!

# There are TWO types of branch deletion :-
1. Delete Local Branch :-
git branch -d feature-login

IMPORTANT : -d works only if : ✅ branch already merged
Otherwise Git protects you.

If branch not merged:
Force Delete (Dangerous) :-
git branch -D feature-login
Capital -D = force delete.

2. Delete Remote Branch (GitHub) :-
To remove branch from GitHub:

git push origin --delete feature-login

<<<<<<< HEAD
This deletes : feature-login, from GitHub remote repo.

# GitHub Pull Request Workflow (PR Workflow) :-

A Pull Request means :
“Please review my branch and merge it into another branch.”
Usually : feature branch → main branch

# MOST IMPORTANT UNDERSTANDING :

PR is basically : a controlled merge request
with : review, discussion, testing, approval
before main gets modified.

# REAL INDUSTRY FLOW :-
git checkout -b feature
↓
write code
↓
git add .
git commit
↓
git push origin feature
↓
Open PR
↓
Review
↓
Merge PR
↓
git checkout main
git pull

# THIS is the standard workflow :-
Create feature branch
↓
Write code there
↓
Push feature branch
↓
Open Pull Request
↓
Team lead reviews
↓
Checks/tests run
↓
Conflicts resolved if any
↓
PR merged into main

# Real Visualization :-
main
│
├── feature-auth
├── feature-payment
├── feature-dashboard

Each developer works separately, Then PR merges features one-by-one safely.
Because : main branch should be protected.

# GIT RESET :- 
What is git reset?

git reset means: “Move Git back to an older state.”

Depending on type, it can : unstage files, remove commits, remove changes completely

# THREE TYPES OF RESET :- 

Reset Type	             What Happens
--soft	              remove commit only
--mixed	              remove commit + unstage
--hard	              remove EVERYTHING

SOFT RESET 
1. git reset --soft HEAD~1 :-
Meaning : remove last commit BUT keep all code and changes staged.  
USE CASE :-
Suppose : wrong commit message, then you can change through this.

MIXED RESET
2. git reset HEAD~1 :-
It means : git reset --mixed HEAD~1
Meaning : remove last commit and changes unstaged BUT keep all code.
USE CASE :-
Suppose : commit was wrong but code still needed. 

HARD RESET
3. git reset --hard HEAD~1 :- 
Meaning : remove last commit and remove all code, changes unstaged.
Hard reset can permanently destroy work.

First Understand HEAD :-
Git keeps pointer called : HEAD
Meaning : current/latest commit position

Syntax	      Meaning
HEAD~1	    one commit back
HEAD~2	    two commits back

# VERY COMMON USE CASES :-

Case 1 — Wrong Commit Message :-
git reset --soft HEAD~1
git commit -m "Correct message"

Case 2 — Accidentally Added Wrong Files :-
git reset
unstages files.

Case 3 — Completely Remove Last Commit :-
git reset --hard HEAD~1

# GIT REVERT :-

What is git revert?

git revert means :-
“Undo a commit safely by creating a new opposite commit.”

Command : git revert HEAD
Observe:
new commit created
old commit still visible
code reversed

IMPORTANT :-
❌ it does NOT delete history  
✅ it adds new history and it deletes previous commit or a specific commit through commit_ID which you can see it in "git log"
Use:
git revert COMMIT_ID

Example:
git revert a1b2c3

# GIT REBASE :-

Rebase says:
“Take my feature commits and replay them on top of latest main.”

# both merge and rebase finally combine code :
BUT The MAIN difference is : Git history (commits)

# Real Commit History Example :
If you run:
git log --oneline

You may see in source control:
M Merge branch 'main'
D Added JWT auth

Notice EXTRA that commit :
Merge branch 'main'
That commit exists ONLY because merge happened.

# git rebase main :
Using the above command NO extra merge commit created which created in git merge feature.
That’s why : rebase gives cleaner history

# REAL COMMIT LOG DIFFERENCE :
1. After Merge :
Merge branch 'main'
Added JWT auth
Added login page
Fixed dashboard bug
Added navbar
It looks Messier.

2. After Rebase :
Added JWT auth
Added login page
Fixed dashboard bug
Added navbar
It gives Cleaner linear flow.

# Merge Says :
"Let's combine histories"
So Git creates:extra merge commit

# Rebase Says :
"Let's replay my commits on latest main"
So:❌ no merge commit needed  

# THE BIGGEST DIFFERENCE :
Command	        Main Purpose
git pull	    Get latest remote code
git merge	    Combine two branches
git rebase	  Replay commits on updated base

# FINAL PUSH CONFUSION CLEARED :
Command	        Push Needed After?
git pull	        Usually NO (depends on scenrio)
git merge	        YES (coz after merge it updates only local, we have to update main also)
git rebase	      YES (same as merge)